# International Day of Women and Girls in Science: The Women Who Planted the Seed

Today is the International Day of Women and Girls in Science, and I find myself deeply grateful for a career in STEM. In this moment of gratitude, I paused to reflect on how I got here. The truth is: many women carried me.

My first real encounter with what a career in science could look like happened during my undergraduate years at the University of Ilorin, where I studied Plant Biology. If I am honest, I was uncertain about what my career path would look like after graduation. I loved science, but I didn’t yet see a clear future for myself in it.

Then, in 2018, just one year before graduation, I had the opportunity to intern at IITA. It was a short internship, but it changed the trajectory of my life.

There, I met Dr. Onasanya Olabisi Omolara, who was doing her Ph.D. in Switzerland at the time and was in Nigeria conducting part of her research. I didn’t fully understand what her work entailed back then, but now, as a Ph.D. candidate myself, I do. I watched her move through research with such precision and intentionality. We went to Odeda and Ido to apply different fertilizer treatments, measure soil moisture, and collect samples for carbon and nitrogen quantification. I saw the depth of thinking behind every step.

In those moments, standing in the field, I knew: this is where I am meant to be.
I wanted to ask questions.
I wanted to understand why things happened.
I wanted to be a researcher.

When I returned to school, I enrolled in Plant Breeding, which was an optional course, because that internship had unlocked a deeper level of curiosity in me. For the first time, I allowed myself to imagine pursuing a Ph.D.

After graduating, I reached out to Dr. Carrie Butts-Wilmsmeyer at SIUE, and she graciously took me in. She is an exceptional researcher and an even more amazing advisor. During my Master’s, I immersed myself in research and loved every bit of it. Although I had always envisioned going into industry, I wanted to explore the field side of breeding more deeply. So, I applied for a field research internship with Corteva. Up until then, I had been working primarily in a corn lab, but I missed the feeling I had in Odeda and Ido.

Corteva gave me that and more. There, I met Dr. Sarah Potts, a corn breeder whose career opened my eyes even wider. I watched the way she drove to various field locations, meticulously collecting notes and constantly anticipating opportunistic data. The passion by which she talked about her job and the sheer excitement she had for her work were infectious. One realization became clear: the women whose career paths I admired all held Ph.Ds. If I wanted that level of impact, I needed to pursue one, too.

I applied to Washington State University and connected with my current advisor, Dr. Kim Campbell, a wheat geneticist. Working with her, it felt like I had been walking down a straight path, and suddenly that path split into two, then four, and then eight. The possibilities expanded in ways I hadn’t imagined. I knew exactly where I wanted to focus: quantitative genetics, the intersection of breeding and statistics. I loved the analytical depth and the power of data-driven decisions in crop improvement.

Because I have always envisioned a career in industry, I applied for a Bayer internship as a quantitative genetics intern. During that experience, I worked with Dr. Christina Finegan and witnessed excellence in action, analyzing data with both rigor and grace.

Throughout my journey, I have learned the profound power of representation. It planted a seed in me, a quiet but powerful belief that if other women could do this, so could I. I have had the privilege of working alongside remarkable women from different parts of the world. Their passion, dedication, intelligence, and leadership have shaped me in ways they may never fully know.

As I begin wrapping up my Ph.D. and look ahead to a career in industry, I am reminded that my story is not mine alone. It was nurtured by women who paved the way, who modeled excellence, and who planted seeds they didn’t even realize they were sowing. Now, both as a scientist and a mother, I understand even more deeply that careers, like crops, are grown intentionally.

I am committed to paying this forward, because my career is greater than just me. Whether in the field, in the lab, or on my computer, I am breeding for the future, both for food and for people.
